<BaseLayout title="Latino Family Center | Community Learning Resources" description="A collaborative community-program project developing reusable art and STEM educational materials and organizing a supply drive for the Latino Family Center.">
<article class="lfc">
  <header class="intro"><div class="container">
    <a class="back" href="/selected-projects/">← Selected Projects</a>
    <div class="eyebrow">Community partnership / 2019</div>
    <h1>Learning resources for<br/><em>the Latino Family Center.</em></h1>
    
    <div class="context"><span>Latino Family Center · YWCA High Point</span><span>Art curriculum · Resource coordination · Service learning</span></div>
  </div></header>
  <section class="summary"><div class="container lead-grid"><div><div class="eyebrow">The project</div><h2>Supporting a program that runs every week.</h2></div><div><p>The Latino Family Center supports families through educational and community services, including English-language classes. Children's art and STEM activities ran alongside that programming, creating a need for accessible activities and reliable materials.</p><p>Through a university service-learning partnership, my project partner and I volunteered during the children's activities and met with center staff to understand what would help most. Our initial plan was broad, including supply organization, campus partnerships, and changes to childcare programming. We ultimately focused on two reusable curricula and enough donated materials to get them started.</p></div></div></section>
  <figure class="lfc-photo container"><img src="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/School_Supplies.jpg?width=1100" loading="lazy" alt="Notebook, scissors, colored pencils, and school supplies" /><figcaption>Illustrative materials, not a photograph from the project. TheUltimateGrass / Wikimedia Commons (CC0).</figcaption></figure><section class="process"><div class="container"><div class="eyebrow">From proposal to delivery</div><div class="steps"><div><small>01 / Listen</small><h3>Listen to staff.</h3><p>Our initial proposal explored organizing supplies, using existing facilities, building partnerships, and introducing more consistent activities. Conversations with center staff helped us focus on what could realistically be completed and maintained.</p></div><div><small>02 / Design</small><h3>Build the curricula.</h3><p>I developed the monthly art curriculum with activity instructions and materials lists. My partner, a STEM major who already worked with the center, developed the topic-organized STEM binder and coordinated related activities.</p></div><div><small>03 / Resource</small><h3>Get supplies in place.</h3><p>I advertised the supply drive, collected and counted donations, and distributed the materials to the LFC. We wanted staff and volunteers to have the supplies needed to use the activities.</p></div></div></div></section>
  <section class="evidence"><div class="container"><div class="eyebrow">What we delivered</div><h2>Materials designed for repeat use.</h2><div class="metrics"><div><strong>02</strong><span>Curriculum binders</span></div><div><strong>520+</strong><span>Donated items</span></div><div><strong>04</strong><span>Weeks of collection</span></div></div><div class="deliverables"><div><h3>My work: art and supplies</h3><p>I selected and organized monthly art activities, prepared reusable instruction sheets, handled advertising and collection for the supply drive, counted and delivered donations, and created and presented our final project poster.</p></div><div><h3>My partner's work: STEM</h3><p>My partner developed the STEM curriculum and coordinated related programs, including activities with HPU's Mobile Lab and Chemistry Club. Together, we revised our plan based on staff feedback and volunteered with the weekly program.</p></div></div><p class="note">The final poster reports more than 520 donated items, while the working donation log records at least 529. We did not formally evaluate long-term use of the binders.</p></div></section>
  <section class="reflection"><div class="container lead-grid"><div><div class="eyebrow">Reflection</div><h2>A deliverable is not the same as a sustainable program.</h2></div><div><p>I was proud of the finished materials, but I also had reservations. The binders could be photocopied, yet the originals might get lost or damaged. We collected a substantial amount of supplies, but eventually those would run out.</p><p>The project helped me understand the difference between delivering something useful and making sure an organization can maintain it. I still think about that when I build training programs, documentation, and operational tools.</p><p class="source">Project basis: 2019 proposal, staff meeting notes, progress report, curriculum instructions, supply log, final presentation, and personal reflection. The work was completed collaboratively; individual responsibilities are distinguished above.</p></div></div></section>
  <nav class="end"><div class="container"><a href="/selected-projects/">← Back to Selected Projects</a><a href="/research-writing/">Explore Research &amp; Writing →</a></div></nav>
</article>
